Create a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script (Termux-targeted) that checks HTML files for tag balance.
Describe purpose, inputs/outputs, dependencies, logic, error handling, edge
cases, and usage, without including code.
Note: the provided code is truncated mid-way through `handle_endtag`, so the
prompt covers the behavior evident from the visible portion and the likely
remainder (reporting, fixing, CLI) in a reasonable, generic way.
Write a complete, self-contained Python 3 command-line script named along the
lines of an HTML tag balance checker, intended to run under Termux on Android
(use a `/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python` shebang line).
**Purpose:** The script scans HTML source and verifies that opening and closing
tags are properly balanced, reporting unclosed tags, unexpected closing tags,
and mismatched nesting.
It may optionally repair simple imbalances in the file. **Dependencies:** Use
only the Python standard library: `argparse`, `contextlib`, `sys`,
`html.parser.HTMLParser`, and `pathlib.Path`.
Include `from __future__ import annotations` at the top. **Void elements:**
Define a frozen set of HTML void elements that never have closing tags: area,
base, br, col, embed, hr, img, input, link, meta, param, source, track, wbr.
Start tags for these must be ignored by the balance stack. **Core parser
behavior:** Implement a subclass of `HTMLParser` (named along the lines of
`TagBalanceChecker`) that: - Initializes an open-tag stack, an error list, a
stored raw source string, and a flag indicating whether a fix is needed. -
Provides a method to store the raw source text. - On each start tag, lowercases
the tag name; if it is a void element, skips it; otherwise pushes the tag name
and its position (line, column from `getpos()`) onto the stack. - On each end
tag, lowercases the tag name.
If the top of the stack matches, pop it.
Otherwise, search downward through the stack for the most recent matching open
tag; if found, remove that entry (tolerating the intervening unclosed tags,
which remain reportable); if not found, record an error entry of kind
"unexpected_closing" with the tag and position and set the fix-needed flag.
Wrap this logic in a try/except that records an unexpected-closing error on any
exception. - At the end of parsing, any tags remaining on the stack are reported
as unclosed, with their positions. **Inputs:** One or more HTML file paths
supplied on the command line via argparse (accept multiple files).
Read files as UTF-8 text, using `pathlib.Path`.
Support an option to apply fixes (for example, a `--fix` flag) that writes
repaired output, and an option to write output to a different file or print to
stdout; keep the default behavior non-destructive (report only, do not modify
files unless fix is requested).
Before overwriting any file, consider creating a backup copy. **Outputs:** For
each file, print a human-readable report to stdout listing each error with its
kind, tag name, and line/column position, or a clear success message if the
document is balanced.
Print errors and usage problems to stderr.
Use exit codes: 0 when all files are balanced, nonzero when any imbalance or
error is found, and a distinct nonzero code for usage or I/O failures. **Error
handling:** Handle missing files, unreadable files, decoding errors, and invalid
arguments gracefully with clear messages instead of tracebacks.
Use `contextlib.suppress` or similar where appropriate for benign cleanup.
Continue processing remaining files when one fails. **Edge cases:** Handle
case-insensitive tag names, self-closing void tags, empty files, files with only
text, nested identical tags, duplicate unclosed tags, and malformed input that
the parser tolerates.
Do not crash on any input the stdlib parser accepts. **Execution:** Make the
script directly executable with a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ ==
"__main__":`, returning an integer exit status passed to `sys.exit`.
Keep code readable, with type hints, concise docstrings, and comments matching
the style of a small utility script. ---
